INT. CORNER LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Fluorescent hum. A row of dryers turns slow, tumbling a rainbow of someone else's socks. Rain streaks the front window. At the back, a payphone with a cracked receiver.

LENKA PAVLOVIC (8, braids, pajama top under a parka) stands on a plastic chair, both hands wrapped round the receiver, a bag of laundry at her feet.

A sudden CRASH from the street. A truck backfiring. Lenka shrieks and drops the phone. Socks spill from the bag. She freezes, wide-eyed, then scrambles down and snatches the swinging receiver.

A tiny voice crackles out of it.

MOTHER (V.O.)
(tinny, far away)
Lenka? Lenka, are you there?

LENKA
Mama, there was a monster!

MOTHER (V.O.)
What was that noise?

MOTHER (V.O.)
Are you hurt? Say something!

LENKA ^
It was a truck, I think! I wasn't scared, I promise!

MOTHER (V.O.)
Stay put. I'm on the bus. Ten minutes.

LENKA
I'll stay.

She hangs up gently. Crouches. Starts to gather the socks, tucking each one into the bag as though it is a small lost animal.
